Learning points
 ► Stiff person syndrome (SPS) is a rare, progressive, debilitating 
movement disorder predominantly seen in women.
 ► SPS can be classic, partial or paraneoplastic.
 ► Patients presenting with features of SPS should be screened 
for underlying malignancy.
 ► SPS can mimic other movement disorders and making the 
correct diagnosis is key to providing appropriate care.
 ► In paraneoplastic SPS, treating the underlying malignancy can 
achieve significant improvement of neurological symptoms.
